"Displays projected observations and variable loadings together to explain a low-dimensional multivariate representation.\n\nUse when: A dimension-reduction model needs interpretation through both records and contributing variables. The retained dimensions explain enough structure for a useful summary.\n\nAvoid when: The projection explains little variance or scores and loadings would be read without their scaling convention.\n\nVisual grammar: Point positions encode observation scores on selected components. Arrow direction and length encode variable loadings under a stated biplot scaling.\n\nBuild: Center or standardize variables according to a documented policy and fit the component model. Choose components and label their explained-variance percentages. Plot scores and appropriately scaled loading arrows, placing variable names beyond arrow tips.\n\nCheck: Check loading and score scales follow the selected biplot convention. Verify explained variance matches the fitted model and displayed components. State that omitted dimensions can contain important relationships.\n\nPitfalls: Arrow angles approximate correlations only under particular scaling and adequate representation. Crossing arrows may be meaningful; prefer a loading heatmap when arrows or labels become crowded.\n\nExample: Explore synthetic product measurements with the first two PCA scores and five loading arrows, labeling retained variance and preprocessing.\n\nAlternatives: Scatterplot matrix, Parallel coordinates, Correlation matrix."
